  "summary": "Agouti (ASIP) decides where black pigment sits on a horse that can make it. One working copy, A, restricts black to the points - mane, tail, lower legs and ear rims - making a bay. The recessive a, an 11 base-pair deletion in exon 2 (c.191_201del) that leaves no working protein, lets black cover the whole body, making a black. It shows only on a horse with at least one E at extension: a chestnut (e/e) hides its agouti genotype and still passes it on, so two bays or two chestnuts can produce a black foal. UC Davis recognises these two alleles only, and tests for them in all breeds. The proposed alleles At (seal brown) and A+ (wild bay) are long-standing names for real, repeatedly described looks, but neither has been tied to a distinct variant of ASIP: Etalon says no formal genetic study is available and that such horses may test A/A or A/a; a commercial At test was once offered by Pet DNA Services, was never peer-reviewed or broadly accepted, and ended when the laboratory closed. Shade, sooty, seasonal change and other loci may explain both looks. They are kept here as proposed, unvalidated alleles, with no outcome. Agouti places pigment only: it has no established effect on height, speed, muscle, bone, hoof shape, feathering or white markings. How dark a bay is depends on a separate, unidentified modifier just upstream of ASIP, near RALY, and on extension: that is the abstracted shade locus, not agouti. The recessive a is described as common in Friesians, Morgans, Standardbreds and some Iberian and gaited lines, without published figures.",
